    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d666">

                <pb facs="1129" n="995" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg995"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">881.00/7–545: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 666 <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-05T13:00:00+02:00">July 5, 1945—1
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6756. FonOff informed substance Dept’s 5371, July 2<anchor n="1" xml:id="d666-f1"
                    /> and offhand reaction was that USSR probably would have to be brought into
                    Tangier’s [<hi rendition="#italic">sic</hi>] discussions.</p>
                <p>An awkward feature about this is that other signatories to GCT of 1923 (Dutch,
                    Belgians and Portuguese) have not been invited.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d666-f1" n="1" xml:id="d666-f1-content">
                <p>The same message was sent at the same hour to the Ambassador in the United
                    Kingdom (Winant) as telegram No. 5478.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
